# Assignment 2.5 — HTML Foundations Mini-Project

## Objective

To build a complete personal introduction webpage using only HTML, with a proper document structure, semantic headings, and organized sections. The final code will be committed to a Git repository and uploaded to GitHub.

## Solution

First, I opened Visual Studio Code and created a file named `index.html` inside my project folder. I used the standard HTML5 document structure, including the `DOCTYPE`, `html`, `head`, and `body` elements.

I added the page title and main heading, followed by the required sections: About Me, Education, Technical Skills, Hobbies, and Contact Information. I used semantic headings such as `h1`, `h2`, and `h3` to organize the content clearly. I used paragraphs and lists to present information in a readable format.

As required, I used only HTML without CSS. After writing the code, I saved the file and opened it in a web browser to verify that all sections appeared correctly.

Next, I opened the terminal in my project folder, initialized the Git repository if necessary, staged the final HTML file, and committed the code using Git commands. Finally, I created a GitHub repository and uploaded the project to obtain a shareable GitHub link.

## 1. HTML Code

Save the following code in a file named `index.html`.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>My Personal Introduction</title>
</head>
<body>

    <!-- Main Heading -->
    <header>
        <h1>Your Name</h1>
        <p>Student | Aspiring Web Developer</p>
    </header>

    <hr>

    <!-- About Me Section -->
    <section>
        <h2>About Me</h2>
        <p>
            Hello! My name is Your Name. I am a student interested
            in web development and programming. I enjoy learning
            new technologies and improving my technical skills.
            My goal is to create useful and user-friendly websites.
        </p>
    </section>

    <hr>

    <!-- Education Section -->
    <section>
        <h2>Education</h2>
        <h3>Current Education</h3>
        <p>Qualification: Your Current Qualification</p>
        <p>Institution: Your School or College Name</p>
        <p>Year: 2026</p>
    </section>

    <hr>

    <!-- Technical Skills Section -->
    <section>
        <h2>Technical Skills</h2>
        <ul>
            <li>HTML - Creating webpage structure</li>
            <li>CSS - Understanding webpage styling</li>
            <li>JavaScript - Learning web interactivity</li>
            <li>Git - Version control and code management</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <hr>

    <!-- Hobbies Section -->
    <section>
        <h2>Hobbies</h2>
        <ul>
            <li>Reading books</li>
            <li>Listening to music</li>
            <li>Learning new technologies</li>
            <li>Exploring computers and the internet</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <hr>

    <!-- Contact Information Section -->
    <section>
        <h2>Contact Information</h2>
        <p>Email: your-email@example.com</p>
        <p>Phone: Your Phone Number</p>
        <p>Location: Your City</p>
    </section>

    <hr>

    <!-- Footer -->
    <footer>
        <p>&copy; 2026 Your Name. All rights reserved.</p>
    </footer>

</body>
</html>
```

**Note:** Replace `Your Name`, your qualification, institution, email address, phone number, and city with your own information before submitting.

## 2. Run the Webpage

1. Open Visual Studio Code.
2. Open the project folder.
3. Open `index.html`.
4. Save the file using `Ctrl + S`.
5. Right-click the file and select **Open with Live Server**, or open the file directly in a web browser.
6. Verify that all six required sections are displayed correctly.

## 3. Commit the Code to Git

Open the terminal in your project folder and run the following commands:

```bash
git init
git add index.html
git commit -m "Create personal introduction webpage"
```

If Git has not been configured on your computer, set your username and email first:

```bash
git config --global user.name "Your Name"
git config --global user.email "your-email@example.com"
```

If the folder is already a Git repository, you do not need to run `git init` again.

To verify the commit, run:

```bash
git status
git log --oneline
```

## 4. Upload the Project to GitHub

1. Visit https://github.com/ and sign in to your account.
2. Click the **New repository** option.
3. Enter a repository name, such as `html-foundations-mini-project`.
4. Select Public if you want your instructor to access the project through a link.
5. Create the repository.
6. Follow the instructions displayed by GitHub to connect your local repository and push your code.

For a new, empty GitHub repository, the commands will generally look like this:

```bash
git branch -M main
git remote add origin https://github.com/YOUR-USERNAME/html-foundations-mini-project.git
git push -u origin main
```

Replace `YOUR-USERNAME` with your actual GitHub username. If an `origin` remote already exists, update it instead of adding it again.

Your GitHub repository link will look like this:

`https://github.com/YOUR-USERNAME/html-foundations-mini-project`

